---
/**
 * What is on in the space today, as a live board.
 *
 * Rendered at build time from the baked calendar, then refetched from
 * /api/events (or /events.json) in the browser so a screen left up overnight is
 * right in the morning without anyone touching it. The build-time render is the
 * floor: if the fetch fails - no network, host down, a redeploy mid-request -
 * the board keeps showing the last known good day rather than going blank.
 *
 * Rows carry a status - past / live / next / later - from lib/day-status.ts,
 * and scripts/today.ts re-stamps them on a timer so the board walks through
 * the day by itself with no network involved. A refetch changes *what* is on;
 * the clock changes *where* it stands.
 *
 * Name, title and description come from data/tools.ts, so this page and its
 * card on the index cannot disagree about what the tool is. The one thing that
 * does not follow that: the visible subtitle is the date, not the blurb. This
 * is a board on a wall - what someone standing in front of it needs under the
 * heading is which day they are looking at, and a description of the tool
 * would be telling them about the thing they are already using.
 */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { localDay } from '../lib/events';
import { toolById, pageTitle, SITE } from '../data/tools';

const TOOL = toolById('today');

const day = localDay();

const heading = new Date(`${day}T00:00:00`).toLocaleDateString('en-US', {
  weekday: 'long',
  month: 'long',
  day: 'numeric',
});
---

<Base title={pageTitle(TOOL)} description={TOOL.blurb} shellWidth="72rem">
  {/* Crumb and freshness stamp sit above the plate, so the green panel holds
      the title and the blurb and nothing else. Both are page chrome: they go
      when the board takes the screen. */}
  <div class="sm-crumb-row">
    <a class="sm-back" href="/">← All tools</a>
    <p id="t-status" class="sm-stamp">Checking the calendar…</p>
  </div>

  <main class="sm-sheet">
    <header class="sm-hero">
      <h1 class="sm-h1">{TOOL.name}</h1>
      <p class="sm-lead">{TOOL.blurb}</p>
    </header>

    {/* The board. One element, one copy of the content, at two sizes: a
        miniature here and the whole screen in fullscreen. Nothing inside it is
        written twice and nothing in it changes between the two - going
        fullscreen hides the page around it and lets it grow, and that is the
        entire difference. Its own greeting is part of it, so what you read on
        the page is what the room reads off the wall. */}
    <section id="t-board" class="t-board" aria-label={`Today at ${SITE.org}`}>
      <header class="t-hero">
        <div class="t-hero-text">
          {/* The full greeting, which it could not be while the lockup stood
              beside it: the name appeared twice and the line wrapped to leave
              room for its own repetition. With the mark down in the footer the
              hero is the only place the name is said, so it says it properly.

              It is the one line on this board addressed to a person rather
              than about the schedule, and the line under it is the only
              instruction - what somebody who has just walked through the door
              does next. That pair is why the hero exists; the date and the
              clock are reference, and sit on the other side. */}
          <h2 class="t-welcome">Welcome to Seattle Makers!</h2>
          <p class="t-sub">Please check in at the kiosk</p>
        </div>

        {/* Reference, stacked: what day it is, then what time it is. The date
            used to sit under the greeting as "What's on today · Tuesday,
            September 22", which made the left side carry a greeting, an
            instruction and a fact, and left the clock on its own over here.
            Grouping the two clock-like things is what lets the greeting be a
            greeting.

            The span is gone with it - the date is this element's whole
            content now, and today.ts still rewrites it by id at the day
            rollover. */}
        <div class="t-hero-side">
          {/* The clock earns its place rather than decorating it. Every time
              note on this board is relative - "starts in 45m", "1h 15m left" -
              and a relative time with nothing absolute beside it cannot be
              checked from across a room. It is also the one thing here that is
              true by the minute, which is what stops a screen left up for days
              from reading as a printed poster.

              Minutes, not seconds: a second hand on a wall is a fidget.
              today.ts writes it from the board's own clock, so `?now=` moves it
              with everything else - a simulated board must never show the real
              time beside a made-up schedule. Empty until then, like the list. */}
          <p class="t-date" id="t-date">{heading}</p>
          <p class="t-clock" id="t-clock"></p>
        </div>
      </header>

      <!-- Empty on purpose. There is no longer a build-time render: the baked
           calendar it was drawn from only refreshed when somebody ran
           `npm run events`, so the "floor" it provided was a schedule from
           weeks earlier, shown as today until the first fetch replaced it.
           Better to show nothing for the moment it takes than something wrong.

           today.ts fills this, and says so if it cannot. -->
      <ol id="t-list" class="t-list"></ol>

      <p id="t-empty" class="t-empty">Checking the calendar…</p>

      {/* Filled by the fit pass when the board cannot show every row - which
          happens in the miniature exactly as it does on the wall, because both
          are the same board at different sizes. */}
      <p id="t-more" class="t-more" hidden></p>

      {/* What to do on arriving, and how to reach a person - the two questions
          someone standing in front of this screen has that the schedule cannot
          answer. Part of the board rather than the page, so it is on the wall
          where it is needed; static markup, because today.ts only ever
          replaces the rows.

          It stays put when the day is empty, which is when it matters most:
          a board reading "Nothing on the calendar today" and nothing else
          tells someone who just walked in exactly nothing.

          The number is read off seattlemakers.org, where the home, about and
          contact pages all publish the same one. If the space ever changes it,
          it changes there first and this has to follow - a stale number on a
          wall is worse than no number, because it is tried before anyone
          thinks to doubt it. Kept as plain text, not a `tel:` link: this
          screen has no pointer, and the people who can tap a number are
          reading it off the wall into their own phone anyway. */}
      <footer class="t-help">
        {/* "New here? Sign in at the check-in PC when you arrive." used to sit
            above this one and has gone to the hero, where it is the greeting's
            own second line. Two ways of saying "check in" on one board is one
            way too many, and the instruction belongs next to the welcome
            rather than in the small print at the foot.

            What is left down here is the question the schedule genuinely
            cannot answer: how to find a person. */}
        <p class="t-help-item">
          <span class="t-help-label">Need a hand?</span> Call <b class="t-phone">(206) 659-1726</b> to reach a staff member in the building.
        </p>

        {/* The lockup, knocked out white on the ink strip.

            The one-line "Skinny" variant, not the stacked one, for the reason
            the site footer already found the hard way: at a corner-sized
            footprint the stacked mark puts SEATTLE on its own line at about
            13px and it goes soft, where one line buys roughly double the
            letter height in the same space. This board is read from further
            away than a footer ever is.

            `alt=""` on purpose: the greeting at the top of the board says
            Seattle Makers in words, so a screen reader announcing the picture
            as well would only read the name twice. The intrinsic size is
            stated so the strip does not reflow when the file lands. */}
        <img class="t-mark" src="/brand/wordmark-inline.png" alt="" width="500" height="63" />
      </footer>
    </section>

    <div class="t-actions">
      <button id="t-full" class="sm-btn" type="button">Full screen</button>
      <p class="t-hint">
        Esc leaves it. F11 works too, and so does a screen big enough to be a
        board on its own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

{/* Global, not scoped: today.ts builds rows with createElement in the browser,
    and Astro stamps its scoping attribute at build time - so a scoped rule
    would style the server-rendered floor and nothing that replaces it. Same
    trap as the injected QR on /labels. */}
<style is:global>
  /* ============================================================ the board ===

     #t-board is the thing that goes on the wall. On this page it is the same
     element, with the same content, at a fraction of the size - so the window
     shows exactly what the screen will show, including which rows the fit pass
     had to drop. Fullscreen hides the page around it and lets it grow; nothing
     inside it changes.

     That only holds if nothing inside is sized in *page* units. The board is a
     size container and everything in it is measured in `cqmin` - 1% of the
     board's own short side - so the whole thing scales as one drawing. A `rem`
     anywhere inside would stay put while the board shrank, the miniature would
     fit a different number of rows than the wall screen, and the preview would
     quietly stop being one. The short side rather than the height because the
     board is previewed in landscape and hung in portrait, and type sized off
     the long axis is enormous on the other one - the same reason the label
     geometry follows the short side.

     Shape comes from the screen it will fill: today.ts writes the monitor's own
     aspect ratio into --board-ar, so the miniature is the shape of the thing it
     is a miniature of. The width expression caps it by height first, so a
     portrait screen does not give the page a 2000px-tall preview. */
  .t-board {
    container-type: size;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    width: min(100%, calc(70dvh * var(--board-arn, 1.7778)));
    aspect-ratio: var(--board-ar, 16 / 9);
    margin: 2.25rem auto 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* Mist, not paper. The rows are plates that stand off this; white here
       would leave the later rows invisible and the whole board flat again. */
    background: var(--color-sm-mist);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-sm-line);
    border-radius: 10px;
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgb(20 25 22 / 0.05),
      0 12px 28px rgb(20 25 22 / 0.08);
  }

  /* The board's own greeting - part of the board, not of the page. Someone
     standing in the space is not looking at a web page called "Daily Events",
     and the page's own title is no use to them. */
  .t-hero {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 4cqmin;
    /* Full-bleed green, but its text lands on the same left edge as the rows
       below: past a point the content stops widening and centres instead, so a
       very wide screen does not run titles into lines nobody reads from across
       a room. One expression, no breakpoint, and the band stays edge to edge. */
    padding: 3.6cqmin max(3.4cqmin, calc((100% - 168cqmin) / 2));
    background: var(--color-sm-green);
  }

  /* min-width: 0 so a long greeting wraps rather than shoving the lockup off
     the band - a flex item's default min-width is its content. */
  .t-hero-text {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The reference column: the day over the minute. Right-aligned so the two
     share an edge and read as one block rather than as two loose facts. */
  .t-hero-side {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: 0.8cqmin;
    text-align: right;
  }

  /* The quieter of the two, and deliberately: the date is a thing you check
     once on walking in, the clock is the thing that keeps changing. Tinted
     white rather than full, which is the same relationship the summary has to
     the title on a lit plate. */
  .t-date {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2.4cqmin;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.01em;
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-sm-on-green);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* Tabular, so the minute changing does not shuffle the hour sideways - on a
     board this is the one number that redraws itself while somebody is looking
     at it. */
  .t-clock {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 5.4cqmin;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--color-sm-paper);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* 5cqmin, not the 6 the one-word greeting used. "Welcome to Seattle
     Makers!" is 26 characters against "Welcome"'s 7, and the hero's left
     column is what is left after the date and the clock - at 6 it wrapped, and
     a greeting that breaks itself is smaller at five metres, not bigger. */
  .t-welcome {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 5cqmin;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    line-height: 1.08;
    color: var(--color-sm-paper);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* The board's only instruction, so it is sized to be read rather than
     glanced at - a caption under a greeting is not what "please do this" wants
     to look like. */
  .t-sub {
    margin-top: 1.2cqmin;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2.6cqmin;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-sm-on-green);
  }

  /* The list owns whatever is left of the board, and never more: a screen
     cannot be scrolled, so a row past the bottom edge is invisible with no way
     to reveal it. fit() in today.ts hides what will not fit and says so. */
  .t-list {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-height: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    border-top: 0;
    padding: 0 max(3.4cqmin, calc((100% - 168cqmin) / 2));
    /* A flex column, so the rows can share out whatever the day does not use.
       A board holds four events on a Tuesday and nine on a Saturday, and the
       old fixed-height rows meant a quiet day ended in a third of a screen of
       white paper under the last one. Now the rows breathe on a quiet day and
       close up on a busy one, from one rule rather than a breakpoint per
       count. */
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    /* The ground showing through is what divides one event from the next.
       There are no hairlines on this board any more - a rule drawn across a
       row is a table, and the gap between two plates is a board. */
    gap: 1.1cqmin;
    padding-block: 1.4cqmin;
  }

  /* ============================================================== plates ===

     The board is a ground with plates on it, not a page with rules across it.

     That is the change this whole section is. Rows used to be white bands
     divided by hairlines, which is the vocabulary of a document - and this is
     not a document, it is a lit sign on a workshop wall read from about five
     metres. What separates one event from the next here is the ground showing
     through between two plates, not a line drawn on top of them.

     -------------------------------------------------- material is status ---

     The plate's material says where the event stands, before a word of it is
     read:

       past    sunk. No plate at all - mist, the same as the ground - so a
               finished class settles into the board.
       later   a white plate, standing off the ground.
       next    a wash plate, tinted toward the brand green.
       live    lit. A solid green plate with white type on it, the one bright
               block on the board.

     Four materials in one column reads as a stack filling up, which is what a
     day is. It replaces the filled rail that did this job before: a 2px line
     is legible at arm's length and gone at five metres, and the same
     information carried as a block of colour is legible from the door.

     ---------------------------------------------------------- two tiers ---

     On top of the material, `data-status` also decides how much of the board
     each row gets.

       feature   live and next. Big picture, the summary, room to breathe.
       queue     later and past - the base case here, because most of a day is
                 one or the other. One line: time, title, and what kind of
                 thing it is.

     That split is the point of this board. What is running and what is next
     are the two things somebody walking through the door needs, and they used
     to be rendered the same size as a class that finished four hours ago.

     Four scales was the obvious next step and is worse: it steps the left edge
     four times down the page, and "later" and "past" want exactly the same
     room as each other anyway.

     It is all CSS off `data-status`, not different markup, and that is
     load-bearing: tick() mutates rows in place every 30 seconds and never
     rebuilds them, so a class that goes live at 2:15 lights up where it
     stands. Markup that differed by tier would force a re-render on every
     status change and take the progress bar's transition with it. */

  .t-row {
    /* One number each, so a block and the column it sits in cannot drift. */
    --rail: 16cqmin;
    --shot: 11cqmin;
    /* The picture's own inset - one number for every tier, unlike `--pad`,
       which is what it used to borrow. Tying it to the tier made the running
       class's picture sit in a 35px margin and a queue row's in 16px, so the
       inset read as a different decision on every row. */
    --shot-gap: 1.2cqmin;
    --gutter: 2.4cqmin;
    --pad: 1.5cqmin;
    display: grid;
    /* Three tracks: the time block, the picture, the words - and the picture's
       track is reserved rather than `auto`, so it is there whether or not the
       row has one.

       `auto` was tried first and is the obvious answer, since about half the
       calendar's events have no picture and the track then collapses. What it
       produces is a board whose titles start on two different verticals,
       jumping by the width of a picture from row to row - and this is read
       from across a room, where a straight left edge is most of what makes a
       list scannable. The tiers step that edge exactly twice, which is a
       different thing: two blocks, not noise. */
    grid-template-columns: var(--rail) calc(var(--shot) + var(--shot-gap)) 1fr;
    align-items: center;
    /* Transparent on every tier but `live`, which is what stops the framed
       plate being narrower inside than the ones around it. `background-clip`
       is `border-box` by default, so the plate's own colour fills the band and
       nothing shows until a border-color is set. An inset box-shadow would
       have done this without reserving space, and cannot be used: the row is
       `overflow: hidden` with children that paint their own backgrounds, and
       the time block would cover the frame down the left-hand edge. */
    border: 0.4cqmin solid transparent;
    border-radius: 1.8cqmin;
    background: var(--color-sm-paper);
    /* The time block runs to the plate's edge, so the plate has to clip it
       back to its own corners. */
    overflow: hidden;
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgb(20 25 22 / 0.05),
      0 2px 10px rgb(20 25 22 / 0.05);
    /* Grow into the day's slack, and never shrink. The `0` is the load-bearing
       half: a row allowed to shrink would silently squash its own content
       instead of overflowing the list, and fit() detects "too many rows" by
       asking whether the list overflows. Shrinkable rows would let it crush
       eight events into the space for six and report that everything fits.

       The cap stops the other end - two events on a quiet Tuesday would
       otherwise each take half the screen. Set well clear of the tallest thing
       a row of that tier can hold, because the row is `overflow: hidden` and a
       cap that bites into content clips it rather than compressing it. */
    flex: 1 0 auto;
    max-height: 20cqmin;
  }

  /* The feature tier does not grow, and the queue absorbs the slack instead.

     Letting it grow was the obvious reading of "the stage takes whatever the
     queue does not need", and on a board that is mostly feature rows it looks
     broken: three classes running at once on 2026-09-27 gave three plates of
     508-559px holding 410px of content, so each carried about 150px of dead
     white with the green time block stretching through all of it. A grown
     plate does not read as generous, it reads as empty.

     Nothing grows means nothing needs a cap here either, which also retires a
     clipping hazard: these rows hold a three-line title, a two-line summary, a
     countdown and a progress bar, and any cap low enough to stop the dead
     space would have been low enough to cut one of them off. */
  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) {
    --shot: 22cqmin;
    --pad: 2.8cqmin;
    flex-grow: 0;
    /* Explicitly, and it is not tidiness: the base rule's cap is meant for a
       one-line queue row, and left in force here it cut three live plates off
       mid-sentence the moment their own caps were removed. A cap on a row that
       cannot grow has nothing to do except clip. */
    max-height: none;
  }

  /* Nothing to show, so the words start after the time block.

     The picture's track is reserved rather than `auto` so that titles line up
     down the board - that is still right for a row whose picture simply has
     not loaded, or which is showing a studio icon. It is not right for a row
     that will never have one: about a third of the calendar is whole-building
     events that reach neither tier of the fallback, and on a plate their
     reserved slot is 130px of blank paper between the time and the title,
     which reads as a picture that failed rather than as alignment.

     Two left edges is what this costs, and the plates are what make it
     affordable: every row is its own object with its own time block, so the
     shared edge that matters is the one down the right of that block, and it
     does not move. This was a much worse trade when the rows were hairlines on
     one continuous field.

     Placed after the tier rules deliberately - `.t-row.is-bare` and
     `.t-row:is([data-status='live'], …)` have identical specificity, so source
     order is the only thing that decides it. */
  .t-row.is-bare {
    --shot: 0cqmin;
    --shot-gap: 0cqmin;
  }

  /* Faded, not sunk. The first version dropped the plate entirely so a
     finished class became the ground - which reads well written down and badly
     on screen: with no plate under them the time block and the title had
     nothing holding them together, and the grey block ended up the heaviest
     thing on a row that is meant to be receding. A faded plate says the same
     thing and stays an object. */
  .t-row[data-status='past'] {
    box-shadow: none;
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  /* Tinted toward the brand, a step short of lit. */
  .t-row[data-status='next'] {
    background: var(--color-sm-wash);
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgb(20 25 22 / 0.05),
      0 2px 10px rgb(19 114 60 / 0.09);
  }

  /* Framed, not filled.

     This was a solid green plate with white type on it, and a whole field of
     brand green turned out to be too much of it - the running class shouted
     over the rest of the board rather than leading it, and every photograph on
     it had to fight a green surround. The frame keeps the emphasis and gives
     the green back its job as a signal.

     Weight matters more than the colour here. A 1px rule is what the old time
     rail was, and it is legible at arm's length and gone at five metres, which
     is the only distance this board is read from - so the frame is 0.4cqmin,
     about 4px on a 1080 screen and twice that on a 4K panel. The time block
     stays solid green and is what carries the colour now.

     The drop shadow stays green-cast, which is most of why a white plate still
     reads as lifted rather than as another queue row. */
  .t-row[data-status='live'] {
    --shot: 25cqmin;
    --pad: 3.2cqmin;
    border-color: var(--color-sm-green);
    box-shadow:
      0 2px 4px rgb(14 77 41 / 0.1),
      0 10px 28px rgb(14 77 41 / 0.16);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------ the time block ---

     The times were the board's structural idea and were set in the same face,
     weight and colour as the titles beside them, which made the spine
     invisible. They are a block now: their own field of colour down the left
     of every plate, running the plate's full height, with the hour big and
     tabular and the meridiem small and tracked beneath the eye rather than
     beside it in the same size.

     One width for every tier, so the column is a column. The hour size steps
     with the tier; the block does not. */
  .t-time {
    align-self: stretch;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: center;
    align-content: center;
    gap: 0 0.7cqmin;
    padding: var(--pad) 1.2cqmin;
    background: var(--color-sm-mist);
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    color: var(--color-sm-ink);
    white-space: nowrap;
    /* The block is one width for every tier, so anything in it that will not
       fit has to give way inside rather than spill onto the plate. */
    min-width: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .t-h {
    font-size: 2.7cqmin;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.2;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /* Small, tracked, and in the label voice the rest of the board uses for
     things that qualify rather than name. It is never the thing being read -
     nobody checks whether a class at 2:15 is in the morning - so it is sized
     for recognition rather than for reading. */
  .t-mer {
    font-size: 1.3cqmin;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-style: normal;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* The hairline token, not mist. A faded plate is about #F8F9F8 over the
     board's mist, and a mist block on it comes out within a couple of units of
     the plate itself - which on the 10:30pm board, where every row is past,
     made every time look like it had fallen off its plate onto the ground.
     Checked at that hour rather than at one with a live row in it. */
  .t-row[data-status='past'] .t-time {
    background: var(--color-sm-line);
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  .t-row[data-status='next'] .t-time {
    background: rgb(19 114 60 / 0.1);
    color: var(--color-sm-green-dark);
  }
  .t-row[data-status='next'] .t-mer {
    color: var(--color-sm-green-dark);
    opacity: 0.75;
  }

  /* On a feature row the block is the height of a plate, and an hour set at
     the queue's size floats in the middle of it like a caption. Stacked and
     much bigger, it fills what it is standing in - and the time is worth that
     much room on the two rows somebody is actually about to act on. */
  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-time {
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.2cqmin;
  }

  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-h {
    font-size: 4.4cqmin;
  }

  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-mer {
    font-size: 1.7cqmin;
  }

  /* The same green as the frame, so the block reads as part of it rather than
     as a second decision. It was green-dark against a green plate, where it
     needed to be a step darker to show at all; against white it does not. */
  .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-time {
    background: var(--color-sm-green);
    color: var(--color-sm-paper);
  }
  .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-mer {
    color: var(--color-sm-on-green);
  }

  /* ---------------------------------------------------------- the queue ---

     The base case, and it is one line: the title on the left, everything that
     qualifies it pushed to the right edge. `margin-right: auto` on the title
     rather than `justify-content: flex-end` plus a spacer, so the cluster on
     the right stays put whether or not the row has a note in it. */
  .t-body {
    grid-column: 3;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: var(--pad) 2.4cqmin var(--pad) var(--gutter);
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    /* Every line right-justified, not just the first. With a long title the
       meta wraps onto a second line, and left to itself that line packs to the
       left - which strands "finished" on the right of line one above a
       left-aligned row of labels, reading as two different rows. The auto
       margin on the title still wins on line one, because auto margins take
       the free space before justify-content sees it. */
    justify-content: flex-end;
    gap: 0.3cqmin 1.8cqmin;
  }

  .t-title {
    order: 1;
    margin-right: auto;
    min-width: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2.6cqmin;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--color-sm-ink);
  }

  .t-note {
    order: 3;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.4cqmin;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  .t-tags {
    order: 2;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.9cqmin;
  }

  /* Two sentences about a class that starts in six hours is not what this
     board is for, and it was what pushed the end of the day off the bottom
     edge. It comes back on the two rows where somebody might act on it. */
  .t-sum {
    display: none;
  }

  /* -------------------------------------------------------- the feature ---

     Back to a block: tags, title, summary, and then how long is left. */
  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-body {
    display: block;
  }

  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-tags {
    margin-bottom: 0.9cqmin;
  }

  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-title {
    margin-right: 0;
    font-size: 3.6cqmin;
    line-height: 1.18;
  }

  /* 4.0, not the 4.6 this started at. With the time block and a 23cqmin
     picture taking their share, 4.6 wrapped "Programmable LEDs" onto two lines
     and pushed the availability chip off the end of the tag row - a title big
     enough to break itself is smaller, not bigger, at five metres. */
  .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-title {
    font-size: 4cqmin;
  }

  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-sum {
    display: -webkit-box;
    -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
    -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin-top: 0.8cqmin;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 2cqmin;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* How long is left, or how long until - the one number on a feature row
     that changes while you are standing there, so it is sized to be read at
     the same distance as the title. */
  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-note {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1.2cqmin;
    font-size: 2.2cqmin;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    color: var(--color-sm-green-dark);
  }

  .t-studio,
  .t-kind,
  .t-flag,
  .t-badge {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.3cqmin;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  /* 1.7, measured rather than chosen: at 1.8 the tag row on the lit plate came
     to 509px in a 501px body and dropped the availability chip onto a line of
     its own, which is the one label there somebody acts on. A long studio pair
     ("leatherworking + sewing") will still wrap, and that is fine - what was
     wrong was the common case wrapping. */
  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) :is(.t-studio, .t-kind, .t-flag) {
    font-size: 1.7cqmin;
  }

  /* What kind of thing this is, and which room it happens in. Both stay plain
     text: they are true of nearly every row, so chipping them would be a wall
     of chips saying nothing - the same argument that keeps availability as the
     one exception below.

     Colour follows meaning rather than position, so the order above can change
     without the palette having to: the kind keeps the green it has always had,
     and the studio takes the ink because it is the fact someone standing in the
     doorway acts on - it tells them where to walk. Two tracked caps side by
     side would read as one phrase on their own, so the studio carries a
     separator when a kind precedes it - drawn in CSS rather than as another
     element, since it is punctuation and not content a screen reader should
     announce. */
  .t-kind {
    color: var(--color-sm-green);
  }

  .t-studio {
    color: var(--color-sm-ink);
  }

  .t-kind + .t-studio::before {
    content: '·';
    margin-right: 0.9cqmin;
    /* Slate, not the hairline token: at 11px a `#e4e7e4` dot is invisible on
       white, which leaves the two labels reading as one phrase - the thing the
       separator exists to prevent. Checked on screen rather than assumed. */
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* Two states get a chip, and only two: a class that is full and a class that
     is off. Everything else on a row is true of nearly every row, so chipping
     it would be a wall of chips saying nothing - and on a board read from a
     few metres away a tinted block is only findable at a glance while it stays
     rare.

     The seat count used to be a third ("3 left", under five places). It was
     removed on 2026-09-22: it is a booking signal, and the people reading this
     board are already in the building. "Can I walk into this" is a question
     somebody standing here has; "how many places are left online" is not. */
  .t-flag {
    padding: 0.3cqmin 0.9cqmin;
    border-radius: 999px;
  }

  .t-flag.is-mute {
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
    background: var(--color-sm-mist);
  }
  .t-flag.is-off {
    color: var(--color-sm-alert);
    background: #fbeaea;
  }

  /* The mist chip has nothing to sit on once the plate is wash itself. */
  .t-row[data-status='next'] .t-flag.is-mute {
    background: var(--color-sm-paper);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------- where the day stands ---

     Four states, and only two of them announce themselves. "Later" is the
     default and needs no decoration; "past" recedes rather than shouting. The
     two carrying what someone walking in actually wants - what is running, and
     what is next - get a badge, and the tier and the plate gave them the room
     and the colour. */

  .t-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 0.5cqmin 1.3cqmin;
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 1.5cqmin;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: #fff;
    background: var(--color-sm-green);
    /* "Starting soon" is 13 characters against "On now"'s 6, and the block it
       sits in is one width for every tier - so at 1.5cqmin the pill came out
       wider than the block and burst through both of its edges, shoving the
       time off the left. Capped and allowed to break, it sets as two centred
       lines in a block that is a plate tall and has the room. */
    max-width: 100%;
    white-space: normal;
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* It hangs off the time block, under the meridiem on a feature plate. The
     block is a plate tall and held one short line; this is the most relevant
     thing that could fill it, and moving it out of the tag row is what stopped
     that row wrapping on the real calendar's longer studio names. */
  .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-badge {
    margin-top: 1.2cqmin;
  }

  /* Knocked out on the lit plate: a green pill on a green ground is a hole. */
  .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-badge {
    color: var(--color-sm-green-dark);
    background: var(--color-sm-paper);
  }

  /* A status light, in the vernacular of every machine in the building: the
     thing that is running has a lamp on it. It is the board's only repeating
     motion, and it sits on the one row that is genuinely happening right now -
     ambient movement anywhere else would be decoration. */
  .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-badge::before {
    content: '';
    width: 1.2cqmin;
    height: 1.2cqmin;
    margin-right: 0.9cqmin;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: currentColor;
    animation: t-pulse 2.4s ease-in-out infinite;
  }

  @keyframes t-pulse {
    0%,
    100% {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    50% {
      opacity: 0.2;
    }
  }

  /* Outlined, not filled: "up next" is a smaller claim than "on now", and the
     plate underneath is already saying most of it. */
  .t-row[data-status='next'] .t-badge {
    padding: 0.36cqmin 1.16cqmin;
    color: var(--color-sm-green-dark);
    background: transparent;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-sm-green-mid);
  }

  /* How far through the running session we are. Presentational only - the same
     fact is in .t-note as words, which is what a screen reader gets. */
  .t-progress {
    height: 1cqmin;
    max-width: 92cqmin;
    margin-top: 1.4cqmin;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--color-sm-wash);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .t-progress i {
    display: block;
    height: 100%;
    border-radius: inherit;
    background: var(--color-sm-green);
    transition: width 600ms ease;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------- the picture ---

     The event's own, from its page's og:image.

     Sized off the board like everything else in here, so the miniature on the
     page drops and wraps exactly where the wall screen does. /api/events serves
     the 768px variant rather than the 1220px original, which the feature tier
     is now the thing that sets: 25cqmin is about 270px on a 1080p portrait
     screen and twice that on a 4K panel.

     Square, and `cover`, because these are other people's photographs at
     whatever aspect they were shot; letterboxed into a fixed box they would
     read as a mistake rather than as a picture.

     The wrapper is what makes the live row's slow pan possible - an image
     cannot clip its own transform, so a scaled `<img>` would simply bleed over
     the words beside it. `.t-shot` owns the geometry and the clipping;
     `.t-thumb` fills it and is the only thing that moves. */
  /* Square, and inset by one small margin on every tier.

     Two things were wrong before and they needed opposite fixes. It was a
     *floating* square - the only element on the plate with a wide margin round
     it, next to a time block running edge to edge - which read as a sticker on
     the card rather than part of it. Stretching it to the plate's full height
     fixed that and broke something else: a square is what a square crop wants
     to be, and a picture whose height came from the row was a different shape
     on every row, portrait beside a three-line title and letterbox beside one
     line.

     So: square again, and the inset cut to about half what it was and made one
     number rather than the tier's `--pad`. Enough to keep the picture off the
     time block and off the plate's edges, not enough to leave it adrift. */
  .t-shot {
    /* Row as well as column. An item that names only a column is auto-placed
       into the next row whose column 2 is free, which - with the body already
       occupying row 1 - is row 2: the picture ends up under the words and the
       row doubles in height. */
    grid-column: 2;
    grid-row: 1;
    align-self: center;
    width: var(--shot);
    aspect-ratio: 1;
    /* No right margin: the body's own left padding is the gap to the words,
       and it wants to be wider than the gap to the time block. */
    margin: var(--shot-gap) 0 var(--shot-gap) var(--shot-gap);
    overflow: hidden;
    border-radius: 1.2cqmin;
    /* Something to look at for the moment between layout and decode, rather
       than a hole in the plate. */
    background: var(--color-sm-mist);
  }

  .t-thumb {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  /* The only ambient motion on the board, and it is on the one picture worth
     looking at: a slow pan across the running class's own photograph.

     2D transform only. `translate3d` promotes the image to its own compositor
     layer, which some capture and remote-display paths render as solid black -
     the same trap the reel's Ken Burns keyframes carry a note about, and this
     board is exactly the kind of screen that gets mirrored or captured.

     26 seconds each way, which is slow enough that nobody watching the board
     sees it move and everybody walking past sees that it is not a poster. */
  .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-thumb:not(.is-icon) {
    animation: t-pan 26s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
  }

  @keyframes t-pan {
    from {
      transform: scale(1.001) translate(0, 0);
    }
    to {
      transform: scale(1.1) translate(-2%, -1.5%);
    }
  }

  /* No photograph, so the studio's icon instead - `contain` and padded, because
     these are badges drawn to sit on a ground rather than photographs to fill
     a frame, and the tint is what says so: a tile the eye reads as a label
     rather than as a picture of the class. */
  .t-thumb.is-icon {
    padding: 14%;
    object-fit: contain;
    /* Mist, not the wash it used to be. Wash was chosen when this was a small
       inset tile that needed a tint strong enough to register at all; as a
       full-height panel the same green reads as a colour block, and it was
       drawing the eye to the rows that have no photograph. Green on this board
       means "on now". */
    background: var(--color-sm-mist);
  }

  /* On the wash plate the panel needs to stop being the same colour as the
     ground it sits on. */
  .t-row[data-status='next'] .t-thumb.is-icon {
    background: var(--color-sm-paper);
  }

  /* ======================================================== dense mode ===

     The escape hatch, set by fit() when even the feature tier will not fit.

     A wall screen cannot be scrolled, so a plate past the bottom edge is
     invisible with no way to reveal it - and fit() may never hide a live row
     or the next one, which are the two facts the board exists to show. When
     those alone overflow there is nothing left to drop, and the old code
     simply let `overflow: hidden` cut the last plate in half with nothing on
     the board admitting to it.

     Real data found it: three classes start at 6pm on 2026-10-07, and three
     lit plates plus an "up next" came to 1637px in a 1543px list. The
     fixture had one live row, so it never came up.

     So the board stops showing the tier rather than clipping it. Every row
     drops to the queue shape - but the plates keep their colour and the badges
     keep their text, so what is running is still the one green block on the
     board. It is the tier that goes, not the information.

     Everything here is the queue geometry restated at a higher specificity.
     The tier rules are written against `.t-row:is(...)`, so `.t-list.is-dense`
     in front of the same selector wins without an `!important` anywhere. */

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) {
    --shot: 11cqmin;
    --pad: 1.5cqmin;
    /* Queue-shaped here, so it takes the queue's share of the slack too. */
    flex-grow: 1;
    max-height: 20cqmin;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-body {
    display: flex;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-title {
    margin-right: auto;
    font-size: 2.6cqmin;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-sum {
    display: none;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-note {
    display: inline;
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: 1.4cqmin;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) :is(.t-studio, .t-kind, .t-flag) {
    font-size: 1.3cqmin;
  }

  /* Back to one line, and the badge beside the time rather than under it.
     `flex-wrap: wrap` is the safety net: a block this narrow cannot always
     hold "11:30 PM" and "ON NOW" side by side, and a wrapped badge costs one
     short line where an overflowing one costs the plate. */
  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-time {
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.4cqmin 0.7cqmin;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-h {
    font-size: 2.7cqmin;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-mer {
    font-size: 1.3cqmin;
  }

  .t-list.is-dense .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-badge {
    margin-top: 0;
    padding: 0.28cqmin 0.9cqmin;
    font-size: 1.15cqmin;
  }

  /* The progress bar is the one thing that genuinely has nowhere to go on a
     single line. Its number survives as words in .t-note, which is what a
     screen reader was getting from it anyway. */
  .t-list.is-dense .t-progress {
    display: none;
  }

  /* A board on a wall is the one screen nobody chose to look at, so the
     preference has to be honoured here rather than treated as a nicety.

     `animation: none` rather than leaning on global.css's blanket reset, which
     sets `animation-duration: 0.01ms !important` - that does not stop an
     animation so much as fast-forward it, and the pan would finish instantly
     and leave the photograph parked at its end keyframe, permanently zoomed
     and offset. This wins on `animation-name`, which the reset does not touch.

     What is left is a board that still says what is running: the lamp holds at
     full, the photograph holds still, and the lit plate was never moving. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-badge::before,
    .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-thumb:not(.is-icon) {
      animation: none;
    }
    .t-progress i {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* ----------------------------------------------------------- cancelled ---

     It stays on the board - someone who came for it needs to see that it is
     off, not find no trace of it - and it has to say so from the door rather
     than on inspection.

     Cancellation cuts across the four statuses rather than being one of them,
     so it overrides the plate instead of sitting in the ladder: a faint alert
     wash for the plate and the alert itself in the time block, which is the
     position the eye already uses to read this board's state. `statuses()`
     guarantees it can never be the green one - see the `off` predicate there. */
  .t-row.is-off {
    background: #fdf3f3;
  }

  .t-row[data-status='past'].is-off {
    background: transparent;
  }

  /* The block is the signal. It is about 170px of colour on a 1080 screen,
     where the chip below is about 90 and the rule through the title is three
     pixels - at five metres this is the one that carries. */
  .t-row.is-off .t-time {
    background: var(--color-sm-alert);
    color: var(--color-sm-paper);
  }

  .t-row.is-off .t-mer {
    color: #f3dada;
  }

  /* Struck through, and thickly. The rule was `1px`, which is a hairline on a
     board measured in cqmin - the same mistake as the old time rail, legible
     at arm's length and invisible from the door. In the board's own units it
     scales with everything else. */
  .t-row.is-off .t-h,
  .t-row.is-off .t-title {
    text-decoration: line-through;
    text-decoration-thickness: 0.26cqmin;
  }

  .t-row.is-off .t-title {
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* Everything on this row that is not saying "cancelled" goes grey with the
     title. The kind label is normally brand green, which on the alert wash put
     green type next to red type - the Christmas pairing the palette note warns
     about, and the one place on the board it actually happened. The studio
     goes with it: it is normally ink to match the title, and a label left
     darker than the struck title it belongs to reads as the more important of
     the two.

     What is left is one red field and one red label on a grey row, which is
     the whole point of the treatment. (The green "On now" badge cannot turn up
     here either - `statuses()` will not call a cancelled class live.) */
  .t-row.is-off .t-kind,
  .t-row.is-off .t-studio {
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* The word itself, at the feature tier's size even on a queue row. Every
     other chip on the board can afford to be small; this is the only one that
     changes what somebody does next. */
  .t-row.is-off .t-flag.is-off {
    font-size: 1.7cqmin;
    padding: 0.4cqmin 1.1cqmin;
  }

  /* A photograph does not dim with `opacity` the way text does, so the colour
     comes out instead: the row's one bright rectangle should not be the event
     that is not happening. */
  .t-row.is-off .t-thumb {
    filter: grayscale(1);
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  /* With nothing to hold, the list must stop claiming the height it would
     have used - `flex: 1 1 auto` on an empty list pushed the message to the
     very bottom of the board under a white void, which reads as a board that
     failed to draw rather than as a quiet day. `replaceChildren()` leaves it
     genuinely childless, so `:empty` is a safe test. */
  .t-list:empty {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }

  /* An empty screen is an invitation, not a void. `auto` block margins centre
     it in whatever the collapsed list left behind. */
  .t-empty {
    margin: auto max(3.4cqmin, calc((100% - 168cqmin) / 2));
    padding: 6cqmin 3cqmin;
    border-radius: 1.8cqmin;
    /* A plate, like every row - on a mist board a mist panel is a hole. */
    background: var(--color-sm-paper);
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgb(20 25 22 / 0.05),
      0 2px 10px rgb(20 25 22 / 0.05);
    text-align: center;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2.2cqmin;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* What the fit pass had to leave off. */
  .t-more {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    margin: 0 max(3.4cqmin, calc((100% - 168cqmin) / 2)) 2cqmin;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 1.3cqmin;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------- the help ---

     A strip along the bottom of the board, in the board's own units so it
     scales with everything else.

     Ink, and it is what turns the board into one composed object rather than
     a panel that runs out of content: green band at the top, light body,
     dark strip at the foot. It was mist, which on a mist board is nothing at
     all - the schedule simply stopped and two grey sentences sat under it.
     Dark also does the job a footer has to do here, which is to be plainly
     *not* another row of the schedule.

     Sage on ink for the number is the live site's own pairing - that is what
     seattlemakers.org sets on its dark bar - so the one strong colour down
     here is borrowed rather than invented.

     `flex: 0 0 auto` is what keeps it honest: the list above is `1 1 auto`, so
     this takes its height first and fit() then hides whatever rows no longer
     fit. That is the right order - a row of the schedule can be dropped and
     counted in "+ N later not shown", while "call this number" has no smaller
     version of itself. */
  .t-help {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.8cqmin 5cqmin;
    padding: 2.4cqmin max(3.4cqmin, calc((100% - 168cqmin) / 2));
    background: var(--color-sm-ink);
    font-size: 1.9cqmin;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: #b9c0bc;
    /* One line of help at the left, the mark at the right. `center` rather
       than the `baseline` this used while it held two sentences - there is no
       shared baseline between a line of text and a picture, and baseline
       alignment sat the lockup on the text's descenders.

       `nowrap`, and it is the fix rather than a preference. The sentence sizes
       to its own content - 755px of it - and with the 227px mark and the gap
       that came to 1036 in a 1007 strip, so the pair wrapped and the lockup
       dropped onto a line of its own at the left. A wrapping *container* puts
       the whole item on the next line; a non-wrapping one lets the sentence
       shrink and break internally, which is what should give way. */
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    gap: 3cqmin;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
  }

  /* A flex item's default min-width is its content, so without this the
     sentence refuses to shrink and pushes the mark off the strip instead. */
  .t-help-item {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Knocked out white: the artwork is black on transparent, so one filter
     reverses it rather than shipping a second file. Not full strength - at
     100% it out-shouts the phone number beside it, which is the thing down
     here somebody actually has to read and key in. */
  .t-mark {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 19cqmin;
    height: auto;
    filter: brightness(0) invert(1);
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .t-help-label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-sm-paper);
  }

  /* Bigger than the sentence around it, because it is the one thing here
     somebody has to read across a room and key into a phone correctly. */
  .t-phone {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 2.6cqmin;
    font-weight: 800;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--color-sm-sage);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* A container query, not a media query: what decides whether there is room
     for a time rail is the board's own width, and the board can be 340px wide
     inside a wide window. Asking the *page* would collapse the rail on a phone
     while leaving it on a miniature of the same size. */
  @container (max-width: 480px) {
    .t-row {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr auto;
      /* cqmin, not the 0.4rem this used to be. A page unit inside the board
         breaks the one rule the whole thing rests on: the board is a size
         container, so a rem in here stays put while the board shrinks and the
         miniature stops being a miniature. */
      gap: 0.6cqmin 0;
      --shot: 13cqmin;
      /* Stacked rows share out the day's slack like the wide ones, but they
         need `align-content` to do it. The grid is two rows deep here - time
         above the words - and auto rows stretch by default, so a grown row
         opened a band of white between a time and its own title. Centring the
         whole grid instead puts the slack outside the pair, where it reads as
         the row's own padding.

         The caps are looser than the wide layout's for the obvious reason:
         every row is taller stacked. */
      align-content: center;
      max-height: 24cqmin;
    }
    .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) {
      --shot: 22cqmin;
      max-height: 64cqmin;
    }
    .t-row[data-status='live'] {
      max-height: 72cqmin;
    }
    /* Smaller in proportion down here, not bigger. It used to sit in the hero
       as the only thing saying where you were, and wanted every pixel; in the
       footer it is a sign-off standing next to the one number somebody has to
       read off the wall and key into a phone. */
    .t-mark {
      width: 24cqmin;
    }
    /* The time block becomes the plate's header bar, edge to edge, and every
       other item is placed explicitly under it.

       `1 / -1` is the correction worth remembering. Left in column 1 it
       stopped where the picture's column began, so a tinted bar ran two
       thirds of the way across each plate and then gave up - which looks like
       a rendering fault rather than a header. There is no rail at this width
       and nothing else in that column to line up with, so the block has no
       reason not to take the full width.

       Back to a row, too: the wide layout stacks the hour over the meridiem to
       fill a block that is a plate tall, and this one is two lines high. */
    .t-time {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
      grid-row: 1;
      flex-direction: row;
      justify-content: flex-start;
      padding: 1cqmin 2.4cqmin;
    }
    .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-time {
      flex-direction: row;
      /* The gap comes back with the direction. Stacked, the feature block sets
         `gap: 0.2cqmin` for the line under the hour; left in place on a row it
         is the space *between* them, and "2:15PM" runs together. */
      gap: 0 0.7cqmin;
    }
    .t-body {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
      padding-left: 2.4cqmin;
    }
    /* The one place the picture stays on the right, and it is not an
       inconsistency: the time is a bar across the top here rather than a
       column, so there is no left edge for a leading picture to indent - and
       half the rows have no picture, which would read as two different row
       shapes rather than as one list. On the right it is beside the words and
       every title still starts on the same edge. */
    .t-shot {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 2;
      /* Flush into the bottom-right corner of the plate, for the reason the
         wide layout is flush: an inset square with space all round it reads as
         something dropped on the card rather than part of it. The time block
         is already a bar across the top here, so the picture closes the
         opposite corner. */
      align-self: start;
      /* Off the tier, like everywhere else. A flat 22cqmin here gave a
         one-line queue row a picture twice the height of its own text. */
      width: var(--shot);
      aspect-ratio: 1;
      margin: var(--shot-gap);
    }
    /* Little room for two tiers at this width - the whole board is about as
       wide as a feature row's picture on the wall. The type steps once and the
       badge carries the rest of the difference. */
    .t-row:is([data-status='live'], [data-status='next']) .t-title {
      font-size: 3.4cqmin;
    }
    .t-row[data-status='live'] .t-title {
      font-size: 3.8cqmin;
    }
  }

  /* ======================================================== debug panel ===

     Behind ?debug=1 only. Deliberately styled as a tool rather than as part of
     the board: a fixed dark bar, and a red hairline across the top of the page
     whenever the clock is simulated, so a board left on a wall showing a made-up
     time can never be mistaken for the real thing. */

  .t-debug {
    position: fixed;
    z-index: 50;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    left: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 1rem;
    background: var(--color-sm-ink);
    color: #e6e8e7;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .t-debug-tag {
    padding: 0.1rem 0.45rem;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: #b03a3a;
    color: #fff;
    font-size: 0.65rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .t-debug-range {
    flex: 1 1 12rem;
    min-width: 8rem;
    accent-color: var(--color-sm-green-mid);
  }

  .t-debug-read {
    min-width: 5.5rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .t-debug-tally {
    color: #9aa3a0;
  }

  .t-debug-date {
    padding: 0.15rem 0.35rem;
    border: 1px solid #454a48;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background: #232827;
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
    color-scheme: dark;
  }

  .t-debug-live {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.7rem;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--color-sm-green);
    color: #fff;
    font: inherit;
    font-weight: 700;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* Unmissable while the clock is a lie. */
  html.t-simulated body::before {
    content: '';
    position: fixed;
    z-index: 60;
    inset: 0 0 auto 0;
    height: 4px;
    background: #b03a3a;
  }

  /* The panel is fixed, so the page has to stop above it. */
  html.t-tv .sm-sheet {
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------- page chrome ---

     Everything the board is not: the button under it and the line explaining
     what it does. */
  .t-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.75rem 1.1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .t-hint {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--color-sm-slate);
  }

  /* ================================================= the board, full size ===

     Two routes to the same picture, and they are genuinely different
     mechanisms.

     1. The button calls requestFullscreen() on the board itself, so the
        browser renders that element and nothing else - the page around it is
        not hidden, it is not drawn. `:fullscreen` is how it gets styled.
     2. F11, or a screen big enough to be a board on its own, leaves the whole
        page rendered. today.ts sets t-tv (and t-full for F11 specifically),
        and *these* rules hide the page chrome and let the board take the
        viewport.

     Both end at one element filling the screen with the same content it had at
     3 inches wide. Nothing here changes a word - the only declarations are
     about size, spacing and the things that stop being appropriate when a
     panel on a page becomes the screen itself. */

  #t-board:fullscreen,
  html.t-tv .t-board {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    max-width: none;
    /* The screen sets the shape now; a ratio aimed at it would letterbox. */
    aspect-ratio: auto;
    margin: 0;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    box-shadow: none;
  }

  html.t-tv .t-board {
    height: 100dvh;
  }

  html.t-tv,
  html.t-tv body {
    height: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* The page, minus the board. */
  html.t-tv .sm-crumb-row,
  html.t-tv .sm-hero,
  html.t-tv .t-actions,
  html.t-tv .sm-footer {
    display: none;
  }

  html.t-tv .sm-sheet {
    max-width: none;
    height: 100dvh;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-radius: 0;
    box-shadow: none;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import '../scripts/today';
</script>
